{
 "generated_at": "2026-10-17T00:43:51.174908+00:00",
 "n_candidates": 1403,
 "question": "Is this candidate pair of planar regions joined by a real press-brake bend (not a sheet-thickness edge, hole wall, outline or artifact)?",
 "criteria": {
  "true": "Yes: the two regions are faces of two different flanges of a sheet-metal part that was folded on a press brake, and the fold runs between them. Typical signs: the regions meet at a clear, non-trivial fold angle (not parallel); they share a long, straight common boundary that is a large part of at least one region's length; the gap between them is small (a few sheet thicknesses at most) and is filled by a narrow curved transition band whose surface normals sweep from one region's normal to the other's; and both regions are real faces of the sheet, clearly wider than one sheet thickness. Both regions may be on the same skin of the sheet or on opposite skins of the two flanges.",
  "false": "No: anything other than a press-brake fold between two flanges. Confusers: (1) sheet-thickness edge - one region is a narrow strip about one sheet thickness wide meeting a face at roughly 90 degrees with a sharp corner and no curved band; (2) hole, slot or cutout wall - a small strip about one thickness wide around an opening; (3) the two skins of one flange - parallel regions facing opposite ways about one sheet thickness apart; (4) one flat face split in two - nearly parallel regions with almost no offset; (5) outline or perimeter facets, a short or point-like contact at a corner, scan noise, or regions that are near each other only because they belong to unrelated parts of the shape."
 },
 "state": {
  "setting": "A sheet-metal part was 3D-scanned and its surface was split into flat (planar) regions by an automatic segmenter. Each question describes one candidate pair of regions that lie near each other. All quantities were measured in code from the scan; lengths are millimetres and angles are degrees. Sheet thickness is estimated per part from parallel opposite skins. 'Transition band' means scan surface lying between the two regions that the segmenter did not assign to any flat region (for a real bend this is the curved bend surface).",
  "judgment": "Decide each pair independently from its own measurements."
 },
